sections.s2_technical:
Dirty checking is the mechanism. When an entity becomes managed, Hibernate snapshots its field values. At flush time it compares the current values to the snapshot; if anything differs, the entity is dirty and needs an UPDATE. By default Hibernate uses a **static, pre-generated UPDATE** that includes every column, because preparing one statement at startup and reusing it is cheaper than building a custom statement each flush, and because a reused prepared statement plays well with the JDBC statement cache.

```java
Account rajesh = em.find(Account.class, "SAV2001");
rajesh.setPhone("9876543210");   // only phone changed
// Default SQL: update accounts set holder_name=?, balance=?, phone=?, status=? where id=?
```

This is usually fine, but it has downsides: it writes columns that did not change (which can trip database triggers, audit-column logic, or optimistic-lock checks on wide tables), and it sends more data. `@DynamicUpdate` on the entity tells Hibernate to generate the UPDATE at runtime with only the dirty columns.

```java
@Entity
@DynamicUpdate   // SQL becomes: update accounts set phone=? where id=?
public class Account { ... }
```

Use `@DynamicUpdate` selectively — on wide tables, tables with many triggers, or where only small parts change — because generating SQL per flush has a small cost and loses the reusable prepared statement.

The `merge()` null-overwrite risk is a different, dangerous issue. `merge(detached)` copies the *entire* state of the detached entity onto the managed entity loaded from the database. If the detached entity is a partially-populated object — for example built from an incoming API request that only set the phone, leaving name and balance null — then merge copies those nulls over the existing database values, wiping them. This is a classic bug when developers map a request DTO directly to an entity and merge it.

```java
// DANGER: detached entity has only phone set; name/balance are null
Account fromRequest = new Account("SAV2001");
fromRequest.setPhone("9876543210");   // name, balance NOT set → null
em.merge(fromRequest);  // merge copies nulls → name and balance BLANKED
```

The correct pattern is to load the managed entity and set only the fields you intend to change (partial update), letting dirty checking handle it, rather than merging a half-built object.

Senior nuances: `@DynamicUpdate` interacts with optimistic locking (`@Version`) — the version column is still checked. Overwriting with null on merge is especially insidious with `@DynamicUpdate` off because every column is written. And for partial updates, prefer loading-and-mutating or an explicit JPQL update query over merging DTO-derived entities.

